CORE
main.cpp
--cpp98
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ nested class reads a private member: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.2\] line \d+ class nested in a friend reads it: SUCCESS$
^\[main\.assertion\.3\] line \d+ WRONG \(must FAIL\): FAILURE$
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
^EXIT=10$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^warning: ignoring
dropped the body
--
N5008 [class.access.nest]/1: a nested class is a member and has the same
access rights as any other member; [class.friend]/2 extends a friend's
access to its member declarations, including a nested class's.  C++98
said the opposite; CWG 45 (a defect report against C++98, adopted in
C++11) changed it, and g++ and clang++ apply it in -std=c++98
-pedantic-errors too.  Following the rule that the front end accepts
what GCC, Clang or MSVC accept, the check no longer depends on the
language mode.  This test used to require the C++98 rejection.
